# cinder_backlight.conf — OPTIONAL override for the panel backlight node and levels.
# Copy to /contents/cinder_backlight.conf. cinder-home auto-detects the common Android/MTK node and
# picks sensible levels, so most devices need NO config at all. Use this file to override the node
# (if detection picks the wrong one) or to pin the raw levels.
#
# ── WHO SETS WHAT ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#   day    the normal daytime backlight. NORMALLY OWNED BY THE UI: Settings ▸ Brightness cycles
#          1..5 and maps onto 15 / 30 / 50 / 70 / 100 % of the node's own max_brightness, so it
#          works whatever the raw scale turns out to be. Level 1 is 15%, never 0 — the lowest
#          setting reachable from the UI has to stay readable, or the screen you would use to turn
#          it back up is invisible, and the level persists across reboots.
#   night  the dim level used while Settings ▸ Theme is NIGHT. Not affected by the Brightness row:
#          night mode is deliberately "minimal light", not "your brightness, but less".
#
# ── PINNING `day` DISABLES THE BRIGHTNESS ROW ─────────────────────────────────────────────────
# If you uncomment `day` below, it WINS: cinder-home stops recomputing it from the Brightness
# setting, so the Settings ▸ Brightness row will still cycle 1..5 and persist but will no longer
# move the panel. cinderhome.log records this at boot ("backlight: day= pinned …") so the row does
# not just look broken.
#
# That is the point: this file is the escape hatch for a device where the auto-detected node or
# max_brightness produces an unreadable panel — precisely the situation in which the UI you would
# use to fix it cannot be read. So it has to override the UI, not the other way round.
#
# Leave `day` commented out (as below) if you only want to change the node or the night level and
# want to keep the Brightness row working.
#
# NOTE: the night dim is NOT resumed on boot — cinder-home always boots at the `day` level even if
# the night theme was persisted, so a daytime boot can never come up too dark to read. The dim is a
# deliberate per-session action: toggle Theme → Night to dim again.
#
# Find the exact node + range in the discovery report ("BACKLIGHT / BRIGHTNESS" section).

# CONFIRMED on-device 2026-07-25 (adb): node below, max_brightness=255, booted at 178 (≈70%).
# This is also cinder-home's auto-detected default, so you only need `path` if detection fails.
path  = /sys/class/leds/lcd-backlight/brightness
night = 8      # MINIMAL light for night mode (1 = dimmest still-on; 0 may switch the panel off)

# day = 178    # UNCOMMENT ONLY TO OVERRIDE the Settings ▸ Brightness row (see above). 178 ≈ 70%.

# Tip: want it even darker at night? Lower `night` toward 1. If 1 is still too bright, the panel's
# minimum is hardware-limited — there is no lower setting without turning the backlight fully off.
